comments[11]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-24T16:12:40.963Z","harness:codex","Intake source: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/1576. Duplicate check: complete strict 3098-item all-status corpus with no omissions plus fresh request-specific searches and current open/in_progress lists; no existing owner matches this distinct acceptance. Full live related lineage was read. Existing delivered lease-fencing/focus behavior remains historically closed. This report is planning evidence; no current independent reproduction or fix is claimed. Queued open and unclaimed under the SDK outcome milestone."
  "2026-10-28T22:03:09.177Z","harness:codex","Additional consumer report: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/1620 (2026-10-28). Its proposed option 2 is the existing expected-revision acceptance here, so reuse this canonical feature instead of creating duplicate work. Reporter describes a separate-process ordinary SDK writer entering between a transaction inspect and apply, losing title/parent/relation/assignee changes. Current source uses WORKSPACE_TRANSACTION_LOCK_ID for the coordinator while item writes take their own lock; the report is consistent with those distinct scopes, but this intake has not independently executed its probe. Include precise transaction-isolation documentation and a native concurrent-writer negative control in this feature. The host-bound recovery delivery only guards compensation ownership; it does not claim general read-decide-write isolation. Item remains open and unclaimed."
  "2026-11-02T13:28:59.385Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation evidence (feat/write-time-preconditions): one expected-revision precondition enforced inside the locked item store. core/store/item-mutation-precondition.ts gained an AsyncLocalStorage revision scope. assertItemMutationPreconditions compares hashDocument(locked document) with the declared item_hash before any write, and advanceItemMutationPreconditions moves it to the scope's own committed revision. Every mutateItem caller, deleteItem and restore (now also advancing) are therefore guarded without per-command wiring, including bulk update-many rows and extension commands that mutate through the SDK. Public SDK: sdk/lifecycle/item-revision.ts (readItemRevision, withExpectedItemRevisions with a checked/unchecked receipt, parseExpectHashSpecs, isItemRevisionConflict, describeItemRevisionChanges), plus PmClient.readItemRevision/withExpectedItemRevisions. Transports: global --expect-hash <item_hash|id=item_hash> (cli/expected-revisions.ts binds after preAction resolves the tracker root and command <id>), and expectHash on SDK runAction/MCP for 19 item-writing actions. pm get reports item_hash (equal to the latest history after_hash), and stdin-json accepts it as a read-only envelope key so get output still round-trips. The refusal item_revision_conflict (exit 4) carries item_id, expected_revision, current_revision (now rendered in the JSON envelope for every compare-and-set refusal) and details.changes_since, the newest 10 history entries with top-level fields. GH-1620: workspace-transaction docs and JSDoc now state that the lock serializes coordinators only. A negative-control spec shows the peer write is lost without the guard and refused with it. Lexicon budgets grow by exactly one inherited host flag (SHARED_HOST_FLAG_GROWTH) with no spare allowance."
  "2026-11-02T15:18:39.250Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 on PR 1654 (gpt-6.1-sol xhigh): all 8 findings verified and fixed with regression specs. P1 CLI binding used a module-global scope, so overlapping in-process invocations could bind into each other; the binding scope now travels in AsyncLocalStorage, and tokens outside a scope refuse. P1 history-redact/repair/compact bypassed the check; executeHistoryRewrite now asserts under the stream lock and advances after its rewrite. P1 restore of a peer-deleted item matched no expectation because the tombstone has empty metadata; assert/advance take an explicit target id, delete advances to the tombstone revision, and restore is judged under its own id. P2 a supplied but empty --expect-hash value silently dropped the guard; it now refuses with expect_hash_invalid. P2 PmClient revision helpers ran outside extension activation; they now use the client's extension context. P2 stale bulk rows kept only a message; update-many/close-many rows carry error_code, expected_revision and current_revision. P2 plan, test-all and history maintenance were missing from the strict expectHash schema list. Full coverage 100/100/100/100 and full quality:static pass after the fixes."
  "2026-11-02T17:47:12.474Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 2+3 on PR 1654 (Codex round 2 plus 13 CodeRabbit threads), all verified and fixed with specs. Deleted items bind through retained history, with the delete's tombstone after_hash as their revision. History maintenance of a peer-deleted item is judged as its tombstone. A supplied null expectHash refuses. PmClient binds inside the extension context and runs outside it, so extension commands dispatch. One expect-hash-actions.ts inventory drives both schema and admission (item-reopen admitted). Store call sites pass the locked located.id. When a peer writes during the lock wait, restore and history maintenance return the typed item_revision_conflict instead of generic drift. Unchecked warnings decorate a shallow copy, so frozen results are safe. Stale bulk rows carry details.changes_since. GlobalOptions.expectHash is documented as CLI-scope only."
  "2026-11-02T19:58:18.431Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 4 on PR 1654 (Codex round 3 plus 4 CodeRabbit threads), all verified and fixed. A guard nested in options (expectHash or expect_hash) is now enforced, via src/sdk/runtime/expect-hash-input.ts, instead of being silently ignored. History rewrites that move the item (legacy Markdown to TOON, or a type folder change) re-locate before advancing. The history context lookup uses the locked located.id."
  "2026-11-02T20:42:53.909Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 4 on PR 1654, all fixed with specs. PmClient revision reads and bindings use a full settings read, so file-backed schema types (schema/types.json) are hydrated and a live custom-type item is no longer misread as its tombstone. Linked-test result recording (test --run, and test-all with tracking enabled) stays best-effort but now propagates a stale declared revision as item_revision_conflict instead of a write_failed warning."
  "2026-11-02T21:39:13.355Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 6 on PR 1654, both fixed with specs. Bindings now store the item's embedded id (read at bind time), and every locked assert/advance matches the entry by either the locked probe id or the embedded id. A write reached through another spelling (a case variant on a case-insensitive filesystem, or a short id) is therefore still guarded. History maintenance gates its extra read on hasActiveItemRevisionScope. An undefined expectHash (top-level or nested) now behaves like an omitted guard: present keys are stripped before dispatch, while arguments with no placement pass through untouched."
  "2026-11-02T22:15:26.001Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 7 on PR 1654, all fixed with specs. The revision-scope store is now one process-wide AsyncLocalStorage registered under Symbol.for('@unbrained/pm-cli/item-revision-scopes'), because the published SDK entrypoints are separate bundle graphs and a guard opened through /sdk must constrain writes made through /sdk/core (specced by loading the module twice). isItemRevisionConflict checks structurally (isPmCliExpectedError plus code), so a refusal from another bundle's PmCliError class is recognized. Delete advances the tombstone expectation through the pre-delete embedded id as an alias, so a delete-then-restore through a case-variant spelling no longer conflicts with itself."
  "2026-11-02T22:53:22.411Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 8 on PR 1654, all fixed with specs. The pre-existing transaction preconditions (validate, committed, historyContext) match either identity of the locked item again (probe spelling or embedded id), restoring upsert_match_changed for case-variant ids. Revision scopes compare trackers by physical path (realpath, only when a scope is active), so a symlinked tracker spelling is guarded. A bare --expect-hash binds to the registered id argument at its actual index (plan add-step <id> works); plan create, whose id slot is a title, needs an explicit target."
  "2026-11-03T01:11:30.208Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 10 on PR 1654, all fixed with specs. A guarded test/test-all --background refuses with expect_hash_background_unsupported, because the worker runs in another process the scope cannot reach. Stripping a guard from runAction options keeps the CLI and literal mutation-input markers and the stdin policy (transferMutationStdinTokenPolicy), so marked inputs like assignee none keep their CLI meaning. Conflict evidence anchors on a before_hash when the matching state's after_hash used another digest (SHA-512), so changes_since is not lost."
learnings[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-02T13:29:25.935Z","harness:claude-code","A precondition scoped with AsyncLocalStorage and evaluated inside the locked item store covers every write path (lifecycle, annotations, links, deps, restore, delete, bulk rows, extension commands) without per-command wiring. The scope's checked/unchecked receipt doubles as a detector for declared-but-unread transport parameters: it caught pm-wsqxwb, where pm_comments/pm_files top-level add silently reads."
  "2026-11-02T13:29:27.511Z","harness:claude-code","Every shared global flag counts toward every command's ratcheted flag-lexicon budget (111 budgets). Adding one host flag means growing all of them by exactly one through SHARED_HOST_FLAG_GROWTH in flag-lexicon-contracts.ts, never by loosening individual ratchets. Global options also leak into update-many's option bag unless invocation-options deletes them."
