docs[2]{path,scope}:
  docs/COMMANDS.md,project
  docs/SDK.md,project
close_reason: "Expected item revisions shipped: pm get reports item_hash, and every locked item write refuses item_revision_conflict when an expected revision no longer holds, across CLI --expect-hash, MCP/runAction expectHash and SDK withExpectedItemRevisions"
body: "## Summary\n\nAgents and extensions often read an item, decide something from it (which PR delivered it, whether its acceptance criteria are met, which checkpoint to resume), and then write. Neither the SDK nor the CLI can apply that write only if the item is still the revision the decision was based on. `update`, `close`, `comments --add` and their SDK equivalents take no expected item hash or revision. A concurrent edit between the read and the write is silently built on, so the agent records a decision about state that no longer exists.\n\nThe lock-TTL fencing from #1549 makes a single mutation safe. It doesn't cover the gap between an agent's read and its later write.\n\n## Evidence (fleet packages, 2026-10-24, pm 2026.10.20–2026.10.24)\n\nRead-only reviews blocked three separate packages on this class in one day, each reproduced against the real SDK:\n\n- **pm-github closeout:** the delivering PR is chosen from the item's `delivery-pr` tag. If the tag changes from PR #1 to PR #2 while evidence is being fetched, the item still closes with PR #1's provenance. The workaround is a scoped `beforeMutation` hash guard under the item lock plus one retry. The API the agent asked for: `close(id, reason, { expectedItemHash })`.\n- **pm-rlm:** failure cleanup read a checkpoint, then saved that stale copy over a concurrent resume's answer, steps and token charges.\n- **pm-slack:** outbox retry cleanup deleted a peer drainer's newer group mappings.\n\nEach package had to hand-roll its own guard.\n\n## Reproduction (plain directory, no git)\n\n1. Agent A: `pm get <id> --json`, records `item_hash` H1, decides to close based on the tags it read.\n2. Agent B: `pm update <id> --tags delivery-pr:2`, so the hash is now H2.\n3. Agent A: `pm close <id> \"Delivered by PR 1\"` succeeds. There is no way to say \"only if the hash is still H1\".\n\n## Expected\n\nAn optional precondition on every mutating command and SDK method, for example `--expect-hash <item_hash>` / `{ expectedItemHash }`, checked under the item lock as part of the write. On mismatch the write is refused with a typed code (for example `stale_precondition`, exit 2). The refusal returns the current hash, plus a compact summary of which fields changed since H1, so the agent can re-decide in one call. The same option belongs on `update`, `close`, `claim`, `comments/notes/learnings --add`, `files/docs/test --add` and bulk mutations (one hash per id).\n\n## Why core\n\nRead-decide-write is the normal agent loop on a shared tracker. Without a native precondition, every package re-implements a lock-plus-hash check, and the races above ship until a reviewer finds them. This is part of making many agents on one tracker safe without git (#1516 measured the throughput side of the same workload)."
